        "alias_analysis": "The supplied complete counterpart list and root baseline contain only Archer 2015. Bibliography and full frontmatter identify two journal reports with different author lists and titles. Distinctness is additionally grounded in the 2016 report’s 6,861-person targeted sample and new analyses, not DOI/year difference. No supplied alias maps DeWitt 2016 to row 5.",
        "alternate_report_analysis": "Archer 2015 reports the 2014 national sample of 3,658. DeWitt 2016 explicitly reuses that national sample and index, but also analyses a distinct 2014-15 targeted sample of 6,861 from 18 schools. For the reused national sample, distribution and demographic results are repeat reporting, not a second independent dataset. However, DeWitt 2016 independently reports the same low/high distribution and South Asian/high-cultural-capital/top-set pattern in its targeted sample as well. Current Results explicitly attributes the younger-cohort distribution to BOTH papers, and Discussion explicitly places BOTH in the retested set. Its new 7,013-person survey measures the corresponding distribution and demographic pattern. Therefore the 2016 report is an empirically tested original paper, not merely the source of an instrument or an alternate title/version of the 2015 article. No claim is made that every 2016 analysis is retested.",
        "closest_chain_analysis": "The current paper directly attributes tested younger-cohort findings to both papers. DeWitt 2016 is not an intervening report that removes the directly named Archer 2015 aspiration finding; Archer 2015 is not merely an ancestor for that separately attributed current comparison. Conversely, re-reporting the national sample does not exhaust DeWitt 2016 because its targeted-sample distribution/pattern is also inside the directly attributed retested set. Count original papers with an actually retested finding, not independent tests or every dataset."
